00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:11.020
a real-time analytics service.

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:16.836
All right, so
today, I would like

00:07:16.836 --> 00:07:20.710
to show five real-time
BigQuery usage patterns we

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:22.300
use in our production system.

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:28.900
The first pattern is complex
query and cache pattern.

00:07:31.850 --> 00:07:37.130
This pattern is in front
of the client application.

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:38.930
This pattern can
be used when you

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:42.170
want to make a little
time-consuming, complex query

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:44.510
in a production system.

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:46.430
Normally, when you
want to analyze

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:49.370
data stored in data
lake or warehouse

00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:53.960
through the production
application,

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:58.340
you put an ETL layer and
fetch data by ETL to database.

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:01.130
Previously, we used
another ETL database

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:03.420
to fetch analyzed data.

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:09.460
But it's complex and not
flexible and not a real-time.

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:12.380
When we tried to change
our access pattern,

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:15.950
we had to change the ETL
process and the application

00:08:15.950 --> 00:08:18.290
call to fetch the data.

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:22.400
So we log it in a memory cache
layer in front of BigQuery

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:26.900
and access it with
SQL transparently.

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:29.390
If you want to change
the access pattern,

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:32.960
you just edit the SQL
ticket the application uses.

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:33.850
It's very flexible.

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:40.200
In our case, we use
hash body with the SQL

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:44.190
take this as the key
of the cache entries.

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:47.500
If the hash entries do not
exist in the cache table,

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:50.650
then it runs the query
actually in BigQuery

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:51.900
and creates a new cache entry.

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:00.500
However, in this pattern,
cache missed query

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:03.560
takes some time, so if
you want to shorten--

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:06.180
to a faster query latency--

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:08.970
optionally, you can create
the cache asynchronously

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:12.500
in the background in advance.

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:14.710
And then, you can
reduce the latency pulp.

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:21.060
This is our actual
production screen.

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:25.300
Actually, we use the first
pattern to show this view.

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:30.020
You can see real-time
data in your application.

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:32.547
It's quick enough, right,
for my application.

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:40.650
The next pattern is ETL
and Simple Query Pattern.

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:43.740
This pattern is
also used in front

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:47.740
of out client application.

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:49.820
When you analyze data
more interactively--

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:51.270
you use BigQuery--

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:53.800
this pattern is useful.

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:56.770
BigQuery is powerful, so
we store our whole log--

00:09:56.770 --> 00:09:58.770
everything-- to BigQuery.

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:01.580
As data log, we're
schema-less and JSON storing.

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:08.650
BigQuery is fast enough even
for these kind of raw data.

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:14.660
But if you want to do a little
heavy task, such as passing

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:19.280
raw JSON, jogging, and
multiple-step aggregation,

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:22.250
it may take more
than 10 seconds.

00:10:22.250 --> 00:10:26.110
If you want to analyze this
schema data more interactivity,

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:28.090
BigQuery ELT--

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:31.120
Save Query-- is suitable.

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:35.320
Save Query is very useful--
and fast and simple.

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:41.470
Save Query saves query
results as a new table.

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:43.510
You don't need any
additional system

00:10:43.510 --> 00:10:49.410
to do ETL on ELT. To
see real-time data,

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:53.715
periodically it extracts keys
and values from raw JSON,

00:10:53.715 --> 00:10:56.950
it calculates aggregate
values, and merges it

00:10:56.950 --> 00:10:59.080
into the summary table.

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:09.410
For example, with Save Query,
it passes JSON raw data

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:13.040
on the aggregates values,
grouped by user ID.

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:17.660
And it creates a row having
segments on stats of each user.

00:11:21.080 --> 00:11:24.610
And from the production
system, the application

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:26.720
runs simple queries,
such as intersection.

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:31.140
So query debt issue of BigQuery
or Single Query is very low.

00:11:33.984 --> 00:11:36.030
It looks like this--

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:37.340
super fast.

00:11:37.340 --> 00:11:41.700
This is implemented only by
ELT-- the BigQuery table--

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:42.200
simply.

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:52.140
This next pattern is Frequent
Update Large Row Pattern.

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:54.790
This pattern is used
in streaming insert

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:55.580
or with user data.

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:02.020
When you query for
the latest row data,

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:04.330
this pattern is useful.

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:10.690
If the average row size and
cardinality are not so large,

00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:12.460
you just insert all rows.

00:12:12.460 --> 00:12:16.930
And you query the latest row
by using the STRING_AGG()

00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:19.030
or ROW_NUMBER().

00:12:19.030 --> 00:12:22.920
But if the average row size
and the cardinality are large,

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:26.210
the cost including streaming
insert cost, storage cost,

00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:29.890
and scan cost becomes high.

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:32.950
So we put in a Buffer
Queue to reduce cardinality

00:12:32.950 --> 00:12:37.040
in front of streaming insert.

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:40.060
Buffer Queue stores
data to be inserted

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:42.270
in concrete the short term.

00:12:42.270 --> 00:12:43.690
Let's say three minutes--

00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:48.380
it's three minutes
since the first entry.

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:51.520
And if we a row having
the same key comes,

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:55.780
it just overrides
the entry's value.

00:12:55.780 --> 00:12:59.340
After the three minutes
since the first entry,

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:04.240
the entry is inserted to
BigQuery with streaming insert.

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:10.040
By using this Buffer Queue, it
prevents to write unnecessarily

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:13.040
all the loads to BigQuery.

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:15.720
Buffer Queue can be
implemented by data flow

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:18.470
or in memory queue.

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:21.420
Actually, we use in
memory rescue, LSQ.

00:13:26.170 --> 00:13:30.930
In our case, we use this pattern
for analyzed user state data.

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:36.080
The average raw data size
is about 200 kilobytes.

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:40.800
And we set buffering
time to five minutes.

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:45.560
It inserts 800 terabytes
per month with this pattern.

00:13:45.560 --> 00:13:48.300
If we didn't apply this
pattern, the data size

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:50.930
would become 10 times more.

00:13:50.930 --> 00:13:54.590
So by using this pattern,
we reduce the cost by 90%.

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:03.930
The fourth pattern is Optimize
for Low-Latency Serving

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:04.430
Pattern.

00:14:07.350 --> 00:14:09.660
This pattern is used for
latency-sensitive layers.

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:16.120
When you want to
use data in BigQuery

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:20.600
in a latency-sensitive system,
you can use this pattern.

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:24.600
Dataflow can easily
export data in BigQuery

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:28.910
to a distributed data store
service, including Spanner,

00:14:28.910 --> 00:14:32.360
Bigtable, GCS, and Redis.

00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:36.690
In our case, we export the
data to Spanner and Bigtable

00:14:36.690 --> 00:14:41.060
to use in the
latency-sensitive layer.

00:14:41.060 --> 00:14:45.440
For example, we export such
as membership information,

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:49.010
recommending information--

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:52.100
stored in BigQuery-- to
graph the user's state

00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:55.420
or make recommendation actions.

00:14:55.420 --> 00:14:58.470
Spanner and Bigtable are
key-body based data store.

00:14:58.470 --> 00:15:02.870
So if a scope of entries
to be fetched it is small,

00:15:02.870 --> 00:15:05.670
the latency becomes very low.

00:15:05.670 --> 00:15:10.490
So if we want to use data
in a low-latency system,

00:15:10.490 --> 00:15:16.370
it is reasonable to export
data to these key-body based

00:15:16.370 --> 00:15:17.070
data store.

00:15:21.210 --> 00:15:23.500
The last pattern is the
Direct Join Pattern.

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:31.790
This pattern uses the Client
Data Management layer.

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:42.060
If your customer has some
real-time data in BigQuery,

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:48.420
you can directory join it to
your data set over a project.

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:52.180
If your data is real-time
and the customer's data

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:58.620
is real-time, then JOINing
the data is also real-time.

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:01.630
You can't control access
rights in data sets, however.

00:16:01.630 --> 00:16:07.770
So you should separate the
data by each customer to share.

00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:09.810
Once the data sets
are shared, then you

00:16:09.810 --> 00:16:15.416
can share the data set
within SQL directly.

00:16:15.416 --> 00:16:19.800
You don't need any operation
cost for import and export.

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:23.880
If you use a BigQuery flat
rate, zero additional cost

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:30.630
is required where is data
linkage and analysis.

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:33.350
If you care about
sensitive data,

00:16:33.350 --> 00:16:36.030
VPC service control helps you.

00:16:36.030 --> 00:16:42.020
It can control access rights
of the data in GCP services

00:16:42.020 --> 00:16:43.430
at a fine-grained level.

00:16:47.890 --> 00:16:51.580
Direct Join Pattern can do
if the targeted data sets

00:16:51.580 --> 00:16:54.250
are located in the same region.

00:16:54.250 --> 00:16:57.800
In our case, on the other hand,
some BigQuery Tokyo-region

00:16:57.800 --> 00:16:58.400
users--

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:01.780
including government,
financial services,

00:17:01.780 --> 00:17:03.910
and banking services--
is restricted

00:17:03.910 --> 00:17:07.780
to store data in Japan
by the company policy.

00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:10.720
And they can never
export sensitive data

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:14.829
to foreign countries.

00:17:14.829 --> 00:17:17.710
In this case, we copy
data from a global region

00:17:17.710 --> 00:17:21.280
to a restricted region or
insert with streaming insert

00:17:21.280 --> 00:17:25.000
to the restricted
region in advance.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:30.520
We contract the US region and
Tokyo region at flat rate.

00:17:30.520 --> 00:17:33.850
We analyze data in the
US region basically.

00:17:33.850 --> 00:17:38.500
And when we want to
analyze sensitive data,

00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:40.480
we analyze in Tokyo region.

00:17:44.690 --> 00:17:48.320
OK, I just shared our
five real-time patterns

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:52.190
we use in production systems.

00:17:52.190 --> 00:17:54.200
This is the last
slide in my part.

00:17:54.200 --> 00:17:59.210
BigQuery is the core of
our analytics architecture

00:17:59.210 --> 00:18:00.480
as I showed.

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:05.370
And it is not only large-scale
and high-throughput data

00:18:05.370 --> 00:18:06.230
engine.

00:18:06.230 --> 00:18:08.330
But also, it can play
an important role

00:18:08.330 --> 00:18:11.120
in a real-time system.

00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:17.110
And it is suitable for
global-scale service

00:18:17.110 --> 00:18:18.740
like KARTE.

00:18:18.740 --> 00:18:21.920
Now it is the center of
the data ecosystem for us

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:25.960
and also our customers.

00:18:25.960 --> 00:18:26.710
That's it.

00:18:26.710 --> 00:18:28.494
Thank you.

00:18:28.494 --> 00:18:31.362
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:35.190 --> 00:18:37.520
TINO TERESHKO: Thank you, Yuki.

00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:43.550
Thanks for team PLAID coming
out-- all the way from Tokyo--

00:18:43.550 --> 00:18:46.430
to share their story
and share what they've

00:18:46.430 --> 00:18:47.610
done over the past years.

00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:51.140
But PLAID started out
with Google and BigQuery

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:52.100
four years ago.

00:18:52.100 --> 00:18:56.090
And they didn't start
with-- as you saw--

00:18:56.090 --> 00:18:58.830
the massive architecture
that they have today.

00:18:58.830 --> 00:18:59.930
They started slow.

00:18:59.930 --> 00:19:04.430
And that's really the message
that I want to tell you guys.

00:19:04.430 --> 00:19:07.280
It's that you don't
have to re-platform.

00:19:07.280 --> 00:19:08.930
You don't have to
re-architect things.

00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:10.700
Real-time doesn't
have to be hard.

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:13.730
You can start with a
very basic architecture.

00:19:13.730 --> 00:19:15.580
If you're building a
brand new architecture,

00:19:15.580 --> 00:19:18.080
it doesn't have to be difficult.
And if you have an existing

00:19:18.080 --> 00:19:22.100
environment, you
can simply just put

00:19:22.100 --> 00:19:26.450
this on top of that-- so
very minimal disruption

00:19:26.450 --> 00:19:27.740
of your existing environment.

00:19:27.740 --> 00:19:32.810
And this is actually probably
a top-three architecture

00:19:32.810 --> 00:19:35.630
for real-time
analytics at Google.

00:19:35.630 --> 00:19:36.420
That's it.

00:19:36.420 --> 00:19:36.920
Right?

00:19:36.920 --> 00:19:40.040
Going from your application,
using BigQuery's streaming

00:19:40.040 --> 00:19:42.370
API-- which is awesome--
straight into the query.

00:19:42.370 --> 00:19:44.750
And your data is
available in real-time.

00:19:44.750 --> 00:19:47.150
And a lot of our
customers do just that.

00:19:47.150 --> 00:19:49.250
And that's perfectly acceptable.

00:19:49.250 --> 00:19:53.240
But our services
work together well.

00:19:53.240 --> 00:19:55.010
And everything's extendable.

00:19:55.010 --> 00:19:59.370
So you can continue to
expand if you wish to do so.

00:19:59.370 --> 00:20:02.750
So for example, if you have
IoT use cases, or mobile--

00:20:02.750 --> 00:20:05.630
maybe you have gaming, logs,
and so on and so forth--

00:20:05.630 --> 00:20:07.250
maybe it makes sense
for you to keep

00:20:07.250 --> 00:20:10.610
some intermediate state
that passes information back

00:20:10.610 --> 00:20:13.130
to the end device.

00:20:13.130 --> 00:20:14.630
In which case, you
might want to use

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:16.520
a high-performance,
low-latency database

00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:18.618
like Bigtable or Firestore.

00:20:18.618 --> 00:20:20.660
We actually built integration
with both of those.

00:20:20.660 --> 00:20:24.035
You can actually query Bigtable
data directly from BigQuery.

00:20:24.035 --> 00:20:25.910
But that would mean
moving it into the cloud.

00:20:25.910 --> 00:20:28.550
And on top of that, as
you saw width PLAID,

00:20:28.550 --> 00:20:32.660
you can start using BigQuery
as an ETL or ELT tool itself.

00:20:32.660 --> 00:20:35.480
You can move data from
left to right, cleanse it,

00:20:35.480 --> 00:20:39.090
enrich it in BigQuery itself.

00:20:39.090 --> 00:20:42.220
The next stage, potentially,
if you so desire

00:20:42.220 --> 00:20:45.450
is to start thinking about a
true real-time architecture.

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:48.350
So you can start thinking
about putting a Cloud Pub/Sub

00:20:48.350 --> 00:20:51.760
or Confluent Kafka
in your architecture

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:53.645
as the shock absorber.

00:20:53.645 --> 00:20:55.520
And then, you can use
our excellent service--

00:20:55.520 --> 00:20:59.045
Google Cloud Dataflow-- or
you can use Hadoop or Spark

00:20:59.045 --> 00:21:02.870
or Spark specifically on
Dataproc for real-time data

00:21:02.870 --> 00:21:03.560
processing.

00:21:03.560 --> 00:21:07.040
To enrich data, to cleanse it,
to create reports in real-time.

00:21:07.040 --> 00:21:09.860
Moving that data into BigQuery
using a streaming API again

00:21:09.860 --> 00:21:11.640
and potentially even
cleansing it further.

00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:13.990
So things are getting
more interesting.

00:21:13.990 --> 00:21:18.980
And as you develop, there's
more and more options.

00:21:18.980 --> 00:21:22.190
You can take your data
from left to right.

00:21:22.190 --> 00:21:25.760
And finally if you
start to really want

00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:27.830
a productize your
architecture, we

00:21:27.830 --> 00:21:33.300
have services that allow you to
control your security, privacy,

00:21:33.300 --> 00:21:36.350
access controls, network,
and so on and so forth--

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:38.060
and including orchestration.

00:21:38.060 --> 00:21:42.620
So all of these things are
fairly flexible, right?

00:21:42.620 --> 00:21:46.220
But let's talk about one
specific pattern here.

00:21:46.220 --> 00:21:49.240
And that is once your data
is in BigQuery storage,

00:21:49.240 --> 00:21:50.240
what can you do with it?

00:21:50.240 --> 00:21:53.390
Especially what
can you do with it

00:21:53.390 --> 00:21:57.260
that doesn't have to do with the
things that BigQuery provides?

00:21:57.260 --> 00:22:00.278
So we've built standard
SQL with lots and lots

00:22:00.278 --> 00:22:02.570
of extensions that allow you
to express your logic just

00:22:02.570 --> 00:22:03.723
in plain SQL.

00:22:03.723 --> 00:22:06.140
We've built UDFs-- user defined
functions-- that allow you

00:22:06.140 --> 00:22:08.030
to go a little bit beyond that.

00:22:08.030 --> 00:22:12.140
We also introduced declarative
BigQuery Machine Learning

00:22:12.140 --> 00:22:16.020
in SQL and the geospatial
capability in the last year.

00:22:16.020 --> 00:22:17.450
And so, all these
things allow you

00:22:17.450 --> 00:22:20.060
to do a lot of things
on BigQuery storage--

00:22:20.060 --> 00:22:25.652
but as long as you are bound
by these processing terms.

00:22:25.652 --> 00:22:27.110
But of course,
you're going to say,

00:22:27.110 --> 00:22:30.193
Tino, I have lots
of other things

00:22:30.193 --> 00:22:31.985
I want to do with my
data, but it's already

00:22:31.985 --> 00:22:33.170
in BigQuery storage.

00:22:33.170 --> 00:22:34.620
What are my options?

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:36.320
So whether you're
using languages

00:22:36.320 --> 00:22:38.390
like Python, Java,
or Go, maybe you

00:22:38.390 --> 00:22:40.730
have a Jupyter
notebook or a Zeppelin.

00:22:40.730 --> 00:22:44.960
Maybe you're using different
libraries like Pandas or Keras

00:22:44.960 --> 00:22:46.490
or different
distributor processing

00:22:46.490 --> 00:22:48.140
frameworks like Spark or Flink.

00:22:48.140 --> 00:22:49.118
What are your options?

00:22:49.118 --> 00:22:50.910
The data is already in
your data warehouse.

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:52.800
What can you do with it?

00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:54.530
Well, there's a couple of them.

00:22:54.530 --> 00:22:58.670
One is maybe your data warehouse
allows you to put agents

00:22:58.670 --> 00:23:01.610
into the data warehouse--
you can run third-party code

00:23:01.610 --> 00:23:02.870
in there.

00:23:02.870 --> 00:23:07.620
Or maybe you can read data
from storage directly--

00:23:07.620 --> 00:23:08.840
so trickling data out.

00:23:08.840 --> 00:23:10.700
Or maybe you can
bulk export data,

00:23:10.700 --> 00:23:14.630
which is kind of this
uncontrollable monsoon of data.

00:23:14.630 --> 00:23:18.260
So to recap, the options are
you can trickle data out,

00:23:18.260 --> 00:23:20.500
just by using a list API.

00:23:20.500 --> 00:23:22.610
You're paginating
over data, which

00:23:22.610 --> 00:23:25.040
can be about 20 megabytes
per second in BigQuery.

00:23:25.040 --> 00:23:27.480
So it's real-time,
but it's very slow.

00:23:27.480 --> 00:23:29.030
It's not parallelizable.

00:23:29.030 --> 00:23:32.900
You can bulk export, which
is decisively not real-time.

00:23:32.900 --> 00:23:35.580
It requires a middleman
like Google Cloud Storage.

00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:38.790
It's probably going to
take you dozens of minutes

00:23:38.790 --> 00:23:42.368
to hours to move lots
of data out of BigQuery.

00:23:42.368 --> 00:23:44.910
So maybe it's not suitable for
some of the more real-time use

00:23:44.910 --> 00:23:45.670
cases.

00:23:45.670 --> 00:23:47.760
And finally, we're
not going to let

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:50.700
you have third-party
agents running in BigQuery.

00:23:50.700 --> 00:23:52.650
It's just the security no-no.

00:23:52.650 --> 00:23:55.980
It's just something
that we can't do.

00:23:55.980 --> 00:23:58.530
So no good options, right?

00:23:58.530 --> 00:24:00.460
And ultimately, when
you think about it,

00:24:00.460 --> 00:24:02.700
your data warehouse
storage, typically,

00:24:02.700 --> 00:24:04.380
is kind of locked out.

00:24:04.380 --> 00:24:07.050
Your storage is locked
away behind a castle

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:10.660
with a huge wall and
a moat around it.

00:24:10.660 --> 00:24:14.520
And yeah, you don't
have a lot of options.

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:16.740
I guess-- a quick segue--

00:24:16.740 --> 00:24:21.220
I was going to use a real
image for this slide,

00:24:21.220 --> 00:24:23.400
but I couldn't get rights to it.

00:24:23.400 --> 00:24:26.210
So I went on Twitter last week,
and I asked folks [? thorata ?]

00:24:26.210 --> 00:24:27.607
to draw a picture.

00:24:27.607 --> 00:24:29.190
And this is the
winner of the contest.

00:24:32.000 --> 00:24:33.780
And the good news
for me is that this

00:24:33.780 --> 00:24:36.270
happens to be my boss's wife.

00:24:36.270 --> 00:24:38.220
So it's good.

00:24:38.220 --> 00:24:42.110
Hopefully I get a
promotion out of it.

00:24:42.110 --> 00:24:45.530
But there is one workaround that
we see in the wild out there.

00:24:45.530 --> 00:24:48.080
If you have data in
your BigQuery storage,

00:24:48.080 --> 00:24:50.420
or other data warehouses,
you could potentially

00:24:50.420 --> 00:24:52.340
keep a copy of that data--

00:24:52.340 --> 00:24:53.930
quote unquote data lake storage.

00:24:53.930 --> 00:24:55.010
It could be GCS.

00:24:55.010 --> 00:24:56.115
It could be HDFS.

00:24:56.115 --> 00:24:58.550
You could use Parquet
files or ORG files.

00:24:58.550 --> 00:25:00.230
But this is a little complex.

00:25:00.230 --> 00:25:06.560
It allows you to leverage these
non-BigQuery processing systems

00:25:06.560 --> 00:25:07.790
on top of your storage.

00:25:07.790 --> 00:25:11.000
But it's twice the
complexity, twice the cost,

00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:13.190
twice the operational headache.

00:25:13.190 --> 00:25:15.000
We feel like we can do better.

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:17.570
So I'm going to pose
the question as what

00:25:17.570 --> 00:25:22.960
if you could treat your data
warehouse storage like a data

00:25:22.960 --> 00:25:24.970
lake-- like storage really?

00:25:24.970 --> 00:25:26.690
What would that look like?

00:25:26.690 --> 00:25:29.120
Well, I have an answer for you.

00:25:29.120 --> 00:25:32.410
And we launched something
called the BigQuery Storage API.

00:25:32.410 --> 00:25:34.177
This solves this exact problem.

00:25:34.177 --> 00:25:35.510
Let's talk about that some more.

00:25:35.510 --> 00:25:40.060
So BigQuery Storage essentially
allows you to read data--

00:25:40.060 --> 00:25:42.640
BigQuery Storage API
allows you to read data

00:25:42.640 --> 00:25:46.350
from BigQuery Storage
at low-latency.

00:25:46.350 --> 00:25:48.490
So one second to first byte.

00:25:48.490 --> 00:25:52.600
It's highly parallelizable,
so if you have

00:25:52.600 --> 00:25:54.820
something that's reading from--

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:56.140
that's distributed from--

00:25:56.140 --> 00:25:58.840
BigQuery Storage API, you
will have some really serious

00:25:58.840 --> 00:25:59.720
throughput.

00:25:59.720 --> 00:26:03.140
We measured multiple dozens
of gigabits per second.

00:26:03.140 --> 00:26:04.370
And it's also very flexible.

00:26:04.370 --> 00:26:08.050
So you can take advantage of
BigQuery's columnar formats

00:26:08.050 --> 00:26:09.070
and so on and so forth.

00:26:09.070 --> 00:26:10.970
So it's really useful.

00:26:10.970 --> 00:26:14.680
And it allows you to do that
non-BigQuery compute native

00:26:14.680 --> 00:26:17.943
processing on top
of BigQuery Storage.

00:26:17.943 --> 00:26:19.360
And with that, I'd
like to welcome

00:26:19.360 --> 00:26:22.652
Seth, who is a Developer
Advocate Engineer.

00:26:22.652 --> 00:26:24.610
He's going to talk a
little bit more about this

00:26:24.610 --> 00:26:25.735
and give us a demo as well.

00:26:25.735 --> 00:26:26.408
Seth.

00:26:26.408 --> 00:26:27.700
SETH HOLLYMAN: Thank you, Tino.

00:26:27.700 --> 00:26:28.742
TINO TERESHKO: All right.

00:26:28.742 --> 00:26:31.928
[APPLAUSE]

00:26:33.783 --> 00:26:34.950
SETH HOLLYMAN: Thanks, Tino.

00:26:34.950 --> 00:26:37.320
So those of you coming
from other data warehouse

00:26:37.320 --> 00:26:40.655
technologies, when you're
dealing with your data

00:26:40.655 --> 00:26:42.030
warehouse storage,
you're dealing

00:26:42.030 --> 00:26:43.170
with a lot of complexity.

00:26:43.170 --> 00:26:45.360
You have to deal with
file system layouts,

00:26:45.360 --> 00:26:49.410
how you map storage to
nodes, all sorts of indexing

00:26:49.410 --> 00:26:51.670
and vacuum strategies.

00:26:51.670 --> 00:26:53.610
There's all this
management overhead

00:26:53.610 --> 00:26:56.950
that goes with maintaining
your data warehouse.

00:26:56.950 --> 00:27:01.120
And when we built BigQuery, we
thought maybe the thing to do

00:27:01.120 --> 00:27:03.830
is to provide an
expert to every user.

00:27:03.830 --> 00:27:07.030
And we do that by managing
your storage on your behalf.

00:27:07.030 --> 00:27:10.540
You don't have to worry about
all these low-level issues

00:27:10.540 --> 00:27:14.620
and you can focus on deriving
insights from your data

00:27:14.620 --> 00:27:15.640
as quickly as possible.

00:27:19.230 --> 00:27:21.350
So let's talk about
what it actually

00:27:21.350 --> 00:27:23.600
means to be BigQuery Storage.

00:27:23.600 --> 00:27:26.850
Our primary abstraction
is the notion of a table.

00:27:26.850 --> 00:27:28.640
And when we present
a table to you,

00:27:28.640 --> 00:27:32.900
we focus your
interest on the things

00:27:32.900 --> 00:27:37.070
that matter for your use cases.

00:27:37.070 --> 00:27:38.400
You define schemas.

00:27:38.400 --> 00:27:41.323
So you tell us what
kind of columns

00:27:41.323 --> 00:27:42.740
are available in
your table-- what

00:27:42.740 --> 00:27:44.525
kind of typing information.

00:27:44.525 --> 00:27:47.340
And we have a lot of
type functionality.

00:27:47.340 --> 00:27:49.850
So you can do things
as simple as strings,

00:27:49.850 --> 00:27:53.840
fixed-precision numbers,
floating point, bytes,

00:27:53.840 --> 00:27:55.680
geography types.

00:27:55.680 --> 00:27:57.830
We also support
complex data types

00:27:57.830 --> 00:28:00.960
like structures and arrays.

00:28:00.960 --> 00:28:03.630
You also provide
information that's

00:28:03.630 --> 00:28:05.698
useful for the consumers
of your tables.

00:28:05.698 --> 00:28:07.740
You provide descriptive
metadata about the tables

00:28:07.740 --> 00:28:09.750
themselves-- any
information you want

00:28:09.750 --> 00:28:13.485
to relay to users of the
tables about what's actually

00:28:13.485 --> 00:28:14.610
contained in these columns?

00:28:14.610 --> 00:28:17.100
How should I use this data?

00:28:17.100 --> 00:28:20.225
There's access control policies,
data lifecycle policies.

00:28:20.225 --> 00:28:24.540
So you can decide when should
data age out of this table?

00:28:24.540 --> 00:28:27.810
Am I setting up for a world
where I retain all information

00:28:27.810 --> 00:28:28.890
indefinitely?

00:28:28.890 --> 00:28:34.410
Or do I want to maintain a
sliding 90-day, 365-day window

00:28:34.410 --> 00:28:37.832
that tells me-- as data
ages out, remove it

00:28:37.832 --> 00:28:39.040
from the table automatically.

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:41.370
I don't think about it.

00:28:41.370 --> 00:28:44.910
The final thing you do is
you provide hints to us--

00:28:44.910 --> 00:28:47.370
through the notion of
partitioning and clustering--

00:28:47.370 --> 00:28:50.740
that basically tell us these
are the kinds of access patterns

00:28:50.740 --> 00:28:52.470
I'm going to use with this data.

00:28:52.470 --> 00:28:54.850
And we use that information
to speed up access for you.

00:28:58.310 --> 00:29:00.917
When we look at a table, we
see a much different view.

00:29:00.917 --> 00:29:02.750
So we take everything
from that first slide.

00:29:02.750 --> 00:29:05.288
And that's basically
the user metadata.

00:29:05.288 --> 00:29:06.830
And then we're doing
all the tracking

00:29:06.830 --> 00:29:10.420
for everything else that
happens in the system.

00:29:10.420 --> 00:29:14.070
So BigQuery supports what's
known as a snapshot isolation

00:29:14.070 --> 00:29:14.570
model.

00:29:14.570 --> 00:29:17.110
So at a given point
of time, we know

00:29:17.110 --> 00:29:20.690
what data is available on
the table and what's not.

00:29:20.690 --> 00:29:22.660
And so we maintain
all the information

00:29:22.660 --> 00:29:26.500
about this data
arrival, data leaving.

00:29:26.500 --> 00:29:29.410
We also have to deal with all
the replication on your behalf.

00:29:29.410 --> 00:29:31.660
So you don't ever have to
think about where's my data?

00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:33.340
Is it fully replicated?

00:29:33.340 --> 00:29:35.840
That's all handled for you.

00:29:35.840 --> 00:29:39.140
As I said earlier, the lifecycle
information-- so we know,

00:29:39.140 --> 00:29:40.790
at a granular
level, what storage

00:29:40.790 --> 00:29:43.820
should be arriving
and leaving the table.

00:29:43.820 --> 00:29:46.580
Internally, we also have
a bunch of other stats

00:29:46.580 --> 00:29:52.560
for understanding operations
that mutate storage.

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:55.520
So in addition to
that metadata layer,

00:29:55.520 --> 00:29:58.690
where do we actually put
the contents of your table?

00:29:58.690 --> 00:30:04.110
Where is the data for
your table itself held?

00:30:04.110 --> 00:30:07.410
We've talked about this in
some blog posts previously.

00:30:07.410 --> 00:30:11.510
So we use a columnar format for
your data, called Capacitor.

00:30:11.510 --> 00:30:15.530
And what that does is it
provides us very fast ways

00:30:15.530 --> 00:30:20.310
to access your data, such that
it supports analytic workloads.

00:30:20.310 --> 00:30:22.103
So as opposed to a
transactional system,

00:30:22.103 --> 00:30:24.020
where you're doing a lot
of frequent mutations

00:30:24.020 --> 00:30:27.800
for individual entities, in
an analytic data warehouse,

00:30:27.800 --> 00:30:30.710
you're scanning a lot of
rows at any given time.

00:30:34.220 --> 00:30:43.640
We also, as this diagram shows,
in addition to grouping data

00:30:43.640 --> 00:30:46.530
based on transactional
operations,

00:30:46.530 --> 00:30:49.610
we also group on a higher-level
abstraction of the partitioning

00:30:49.610 --> 00:30:50.970
information.

00:30:50.970 --> 00:30:52.730
And so what that
effectively means

00:30:52.730 --> 00:30:55.340
is whenever we want
to read a table,

00:30:55.340 --> 00:30:57.620
we end up with a set
of columnar files

00:30:57.620 --> 00:31:00.970
that we need to operate on.

00:31:00.970 --> 00:31:05.600
Our query engine basically opens
all these up simultaneously.

00:31:05.600 --> 00:31:09.590
And so what we've done is
we've tried to figure out a way

00:31:09.590 --> 00:31:12.360
so that you can do
very similar things.

00:31:12.360 --> 00:31:13.890
Essentially, what
we want to present

00:31:13.890 --> 00:31:17.730
is the ability for you
to act as a query engine.

00:31:17.730 --> 00:31:19.950
So on the left-- as you see--

00:31:19.950 --> 00:31:26.130
you've selected a
table or partition,

00:31:26.130 --> 00:31:30.060
and you want to read that into
your external application.

00:31:30.060 --> 00:31:33.050
We deliver that through
a set of streams.

00:31:33.050 --> 00:31:36.350
And then we do decoding
on the client side.

00:31:36.350 --> 00:31:40.490
So, in comparison, let's talk
about what you could do today.

00:31:40.490 --> 00:31:44.060
As Tino mentioned, we have
the table data list mechanism,

00:31:44.060 --> 00:31:46.310
which is a paginated
cursor method.

00:31:46.310 --> 00:31:48.350
So it's a REST API,
which means you're

00:31:48.350 --> 00:31:52.580
doing an individual, discrete,
HTTP requests and responses

00:31:52.580 --> 00:31:54.110
for each page of data.

00:31:54.110 --> 00:31:56.990
You have all this op
flow to get through.

00:31:56.990 --> 00:31:59.490
And the data is presented
to you in a JSON format.

00:31:59.490 --> 00:32:03.270
So it's not particularly
efficient at scale.

00:32:03.270 --> 00:32:07.800
By contrast the Storage API does
a couple of different things

00:32:07.800 --> 00:32:09.540
to handle this.

00:32:09.540 --> 00:32:14.310
On the network layer, we use
gRPC, a streaming RPC protocol,

00:32:14.310 --> 00:32:18.390
to ensure that the latency for
just moving data back and forth

00:32:18.390 --> 00:32:19.365
is as low as possible.

00:32:19.365 --> 00:32:21.240
And we use a lot of flow
control to make sure

00:32:21.240 --> 00:32:24.780
that data is
continuously arriving.

00:32:24.780 --> 00:32:27.460
We use a binary
serialization format.

00:32:27.460 --> 00:32:30.270
So those of you that are
familiar with formats

00:32:30.270 --> 00:32:33.030
like Apache Avro,
you will be reading

00:32:33.030 --> 00:32:36.440
Avro out of these streams.

00:32:36.440 --> 00:32:38.690
We're also looking at
other delivery formats.

00:32:38.690 --> 00:32:40.910
So feel free to catch
me after the talk

00:32:40.910 --> 00:32:43.430
and tell me your
favorite format and why.

00:32:43.430 --> 00:32:47.240
We can look at what
we want to do there.

00:32:47.240 --> 00:32:50.330
We also use this
abstract concept

00:32:50.330 --> 00:32:52.310
of a stream to deliver data.

00:32:52.310 --> 00:32:53.900
And that's our unit
of parallelism.

00:32:56.830 --> 00:33:01.770
So streams make it easy to build
consumer applications based

00:33:01.770 --> 00:33:03.390
on the notion of
your architecture

00:33:03.390 --> 00:33:05.860
rather than our architecture.

00:33:05.860 --> 00:33:07.890
We didn't want to ship
a bunch of complexity

00:33:07.890 --> 00:33:11.480
around understanding our
low-level storage interfaces,

00:33:11.480 --> 00:33:13.920
so we built this
abstraction for you.

00:33:13.920 --> 00:33:16.080
So when you set up a
session, you basically

00:33:16.080 --> 00:33:19.290
tell us basic information
about what kind of data

00:33:19.290 --> 00:33:22.680
you want to read,
properties of the table,

00:33:22.680 --> 00:33:25.990
how you want to access it, and--
importantly-- the parallelism

00:33:25.990 --> 00:33:28.990
you want to read it at.

00:33:28.990 --> 00:33:31.600
Even a single stream
is generally more

00:33:31.600 --> 00:33:33.790
performant than the
new method, simply due

00:33:33.790 --> 00:33:37.980
to the technology changes.

00:33:37.980 --> 00:33:42.600
As you are able to scale
up, across multiple nodes,

00:33:42.600 --> 00:33:44.850
you can establish
additional streams.

00:33:44.850 --> 00:33:48.870
And your throughput
goes up as well.

00:33:48.870 --> 00:33:51.260
And you can keep doing this.

00:33:51.260 --> 00:33:53.690
The basic bounds you have
are how much bandwidth

00:33:53.690 --> 00:33:55.610
you have available?

00:33:55.610 --> 00:33:59.240
Do you have the compute
capacity to do the decoding?

00:33:59.240 --> 00:34:01.880
And what is the
underlying parallelism

00:34:01.880 --> 00:34:03.390
of the table you're accessing?

00:34:03.390 --> 00:34:05.510
So a very small table
may only be represented

00:34:05.510 --> 00:34:08.840
by a single file, so you're
not able to use more than a

00:34:08.840 --> 00:34:11.030
single stream to access it.

00:34:11.030 --> 00:34:14.230
By contrast, as
Yuki mentioned, they

00:34:14.230 --> 00:34:16.810
may have terabytes
to petabytes of data.

00:34:16.810 --> 00:34:19.330
That could be represented
by hundreds or thousands

00:34:19.330 --> 00:34:20.290
of columnar files.

00:34:20.290 --> 00:34:22.639
And so your parallelism
factor scales

00:34:22.639 --> 00:34:26.130
as the amount of data scales.

00:34:26.130 --> 00:34:29.760
So the other thing
to keep in mind

00:34:29.760 --> 00:34:34.822
is we don't make you
reason about which files

00:34:34.822 --> 00:34:36.030
are going into which streams.

00:34:36.030 --> 00:34:38.040
We do a dynamic dispatch.

00:34:38.040 --> 00:34:40.139
So that, essentially, all
you have to worry about

00:34:40.139 --> 00:34:40.739
is the stream.

00:34:40.739 --> 00:34:43.650
And we will make sure that data
is delivered to the streams.

00:34:43.650 --> 00:34:46.659
And we allocate
things in a safe way,

00:34:46.659 --> 00:34:49.690
so you don't have to
worry about duplication

00:34:49.690 --> 00:34:52.830
due to the network transport.

00:34:52.830 --> 00:34:55.739
However, you're dealing
with distributed systems.

00:34:55.739 --> 00:34:57.480
Distributed systems fail.

00:34:57.480 --> 00:34:59.110
They just do.

00:34:59.110 --> 00:35:03.560
So how do you deal with that
when accessing your storage

00:35:03.560 --> 00:35:04.790
through this API?

00:35:04.790 --> 00:35:09.650
So we essentially present three
levels of recovery and error

00:35:09.650 --> 00:35:10.460
control.

00:35:10.460 --> 00:35:13.200
So each of those streams
has an offset mechanism.

00:35:13.200 --> 00:35:16.265
So if you have a bad
read, you can basically

00:35:16.265 --> 00:35:18.140
restart at the last
point you had a good read

00:35:18.140 --> 00:35:22.160
and continue on.

00:35:22.160 --> 00:35:25.160
For example, here if your
receiving node failed,

00:35:25.160 --> 00:35:27.020
and you needed to restart
the entire stream,

00:35:27.020 --> 00:35:29.420
that's perfectly
legitimate as well.

00:35:29.420 --> 00:35:32.700
And say you had a much
more catastrophic failure--

00:35:32.700 --> 00:35:34.910
you can restart
the entire stream.

00:35:34.910 --> 00:35:38.120
And we will deliver the
data in the same order

00:35:38.120 --> 00:35:39.950
we did in the original session.

00:35:45.590 --> 00:35:47.690
One thing to keep
in mind, though--

00:35:47.690 --> 00:35:50.340
many applications are
inherently single node.

00:35:50.340 --> 00:35:53.910
A lot of people have tools that
just run on a local machine.

00:35:53.910 --> 00:35:57.010
As long as you have sufficient
capacity on that machine,

00:35:57.010 --> 00:35:59.130
it's perfectly OK to
fan in multiple streams

00:35:59.130 --> 00:36:02.160
onto that worker.

00:36:02.160 --> 00:36:04.530
One thing to keep in mind--

00:36:04.530 --> 00:36:09.210
there is no ordering guarantees
for each element of the stream.

00:36:09.210 --> 00:36:11.300
So you can't say
stream one is going

00:36:11.300 --> 00:36:13.980
to get A through M, et cetera.

00:36:17.890 --> 00:36:20.712
So when we think
about the Storage API,

00:36:20.712 --> 00:36:22.420
we want to give you
the same capabilities

00:36:22.420 --> 00:36:26.572
we give our query engine, which
means projection and filtering.

00:36:30.020 --> 00:36:31.820
If you only want two
or three columns--

00:36:31.820 --> 00:36:33.710
out of a very wide schema--

00:36:33.710 --> 00:36:36.230
we will only deliver
those columns.

00:36:36.230 --> 00:36:38.690
That speeds you up, because
obviously, we're not

00:36:38.690 --> 00:36:40.280
sending that over the network.

00:36:40.280 --> 00:36:42.300
And we're able to leverage
our columnar format.

00:36:42.300 --> 00:36:45.250
So it's very fast
for us to do this.

00:36:45.250 --> 00:36:50.310
Similarly, you can establish
processes row filters

00:36:50.310 --> 00:36:52.580
to say, I would
like to throw away

00:36:52.580 --> 00:36:54.440
any data on the
server-side before it even

00:36:54.440 --> 00:36:57.497
comes across the network,
so that my consuming

00:36:57.497 --> 00:36:59.330
application doesn't
have to even look at it.

00:36:59.330 --> 00:37:00.497
And you can do that as well.

00:37:03.020 --> 00:37:06.670
So as I mentioned, we have a
snapshot isolation mechanism.

00:37:06.670 --> 00:37:11.030
This is sometimes called
time travel in our system.

00:37:11.030 --> 00:37:13.660
So we also give you
the ability to say,

00:37:13.660 --> 00:37:16.930
I would like to read this
table as of midnight yesterday,

00:37:16.930 --> 00:37:20.600
or three hours ago.

00:37:20.600 --> 00:37:23.540
Cases where you might
want to do this--

00:37:23.540 --> 00:37:28.010
if you just recently
did a big, DML update

00:37:28.010 --> 00:37:29.950
and changed a bunch
of your data and went,

00:37:29.950 --> 00:37:32.780
wait what was the state of
that table before I did that?

00:37:32.780 --> 00:37:36.380
You can walk before that event.

00:37:36.380 --> 00:37:39.170
Similarly, if you're
doing simultaneous reads

00:37:39.170 --> 00:37:42.710
of a bunch of tables and you
want a coherent point in time

00:37:42.710 --> 00:37:45.470
for all three of them, or
however many you're reading,

00:37:45.470 --> 00:37:47.630
you can set the snapshot
time for all of them

00:37:47.630 --> 00:37:49.881
to be identical.

00:37:53.250 --> 00:37:55.387
So if I could switch
to the demo real quick.

00:38:04.450 --> 00:38:10.970
So one thing to keep in mind is
I'm terrible at visual demos.

00:38:10.970 --> 00:38:13.960
So what we'll be
doing today is I'm

00:38:13.960 --> 00:38:17.080
going to demonstrate comparing
the use of the existing

00:38:17.080 --> 00:38:18.730
API to the new API.

00:38:18.730 --> 00:38:22.150
And we're going to use some data
from the public data program--

00:38:22.150 --> 00:38:24.120
the public data sets program.

00:38:24.120 --> 00:38:26.800
And in this case, what we're
going to be working with

00:38:26.800 --> 00:38:29.260
is something called the
sample_commits table.

00:38:29.260 --> 00:38:36.040
So it's basically a subset
of all the commit history

00:38:36.040 --> 00:38:41.980
in GitHub open source projects,
restricted to some subset

00:38:41.980 --> 00:38:44.380
of the available projects.

00:38:44.380 --> 00:38:57.080
And we do that because the
raw data is 753 gigabytes.

00:38:57.080 --> 00:38:58.710
And we can demo it.

00:38:58.710 --> 00:39:03.260
But it will be a much
longer demo session.

00:39:03.260 --> 00:39:06.095
This one, by contrast, is
only about 2 and 1/2 gigs--

00:39:06.095 --> 00:39:09.720
a couple hundred thousand rows.

00:39:09.720 --> 00:39:11.950
And so let's look at--

00:39:11.950 --> 00:39:13.750
essentially, I have
three nodes here--

00:39:13.750 --> 00:39:17.680
GCE VMs, absolutely
vanilla, that I've just

00:39:17.680 --> 00:39:21.140
put a variant of Debian on.

00:39:21.140 --> 00:39:25.830
So, to set the
stage, the top box

00:39:25.830 --> 00:39:29.080
is the existing cursor method.

00:39:29.080 --> 00:39:31.660
It's going to move,
but it's not going

00:39:31.660 --> 00:39:32.890
to move particularly fast.

00:39:38.700 --> 00:39:42.520
So, if we switch to the Storage
API and a single stream,

00:39:42.520 --> 00:39:46.290
we're doing a little better.

00:39:46.290 --> 00:39:48.040
We're certainly going
to arrive at the end

00:39:48.040 --> 00:39:49.990
before the first one.

00:39:49.990 --> 00:39:51.760
So let me stop that.

00:39:51.760 --> 00:39:53.770
And let's parallelize
it some more.

00:39:58.630 --> 00:40:03.000
So my beautiful bar chart--
each color is the data being

00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:06.420
delivered to a specific stream.

00:40:06.420 --> 00:40:08.760
So with a parallelism
factor of 4,

00:40:08.760 --> 00:40:11.460
we're moving a little faster.

00:40:11.460 --> 00:40:15.497
We can stop that,
make it bigger--

00:40:15.497 --> 00:40:16.205
add more streams.

00:40:18.780 --> 00:40:22.830
Unfortunately, text demos
aren't the greatest for visuals,

00:40:22.830 --> 00:40:28.060
but low-level storage
APIs don't inherently

00:40:28.060 --> 00:40:29.690
make for great visual demos.

00:40:29.690 --> 00:40:32.300
I'm sorry.

00:40:32.300 --> 00:40:33.840
So let's do one more thing.

00:40:33.840 --> 00:40:35.840
So in addition to that,
we talked about the fact

00:40:35.840 --> 00:40:37.980
that you can do
column projections.

00:40:37.980 --> 00:40:40.730
So if we say, we're
only interested

00:40:40.730 --> 00:40:47.612
in the actual commit hash and
the author's email address--

00:40:47.612 --> 00:40:48.820
the addresses are anonymized.

00:40:48.820 --> 00:40:52.170
So we lose the identifier,
but we keep the domain suffix

00:40:52.170 --> 00:40:55.056
in this data set.

00:40:55.056 --> 00:40:56.110
A little faster.

00:41:01.540 --> 00:41:04.115
And the last thing
we can do is say--

00:41:18.060 --> 00:41:20.100
So this bar chart
doesn't look very pretty,

00:41:20.100 --> 00:41:23.100
because we're filtering
almost all the data out.

00:41:23.100 --> 00:41:24.715
As you can see, we
apparently-- this

00:41:24.715 --> 00:41:26.090
is the equivalent
of a count star

00:41:26.090 --> 00:41:30.090
query on the table where we
say where the domain suffix is

00:41:30.090 --> 00:41:31.750
like that filter.

00:41:31.750 --> 00:41:33.490
So we have-- apparently--

00:41:33.490 --> 00:41:36.190
6,000 commits in
the GitHub data.

00:41:36.190 --> 00:41:38.400
And as you can see, we're
still waiting on tabledata

00:41:38.400 --> 00:41:41.240
to make its way to
the end of the table.

00:41:41.240 --> 00:41:43.070
That's all I had for
the demo, could we

00:41:43.070 --> 00:41:45.010
switch back to the screen?

00:41:45.010 --> 00:41:46.430
Now I'd like to--

00:41:46.430 --> 00:41:47.390
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:47.390 --> 00:41:48.830
Yeah.

00:41:48.830 --> 00:41:52.200
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:52.200 --> 00:41:53.860
Tino, I'll turn it
back over to you.

00:41:53.860 --> 00:41:54.360
And--

00:41:54.360 --> 00:41:54.780
TINO TERESHKO: Yes, sir.

00:41:54.780 --> 00:41:56.688
SETH HOLLYMAN: --I'll
go sit down over here.

00:41:56.688 --> 00:41:57.480
TINO TERESHKO: Yes.

00:41:57.480 --> 00:41:57.980
Wow.

00:41:57.980 --> 00:41:58.560
Fantastic.

00:41:58.560 --> 00:41:59.950
Thank you so much, Seth.

00:41:59.950 --> 00:42:04.980
So yes, I think between hand
drawings of castles and text

00:42:04.980 --> 00:42:07.960
demos, we've really
raised the bar here.

00:42:07.960 --> 00:42:09.360
But the point stands--

00:42:09.360 --> 00:42:11.790
it's a really, really fast API.

00:42:11.790 --> 00:42:15.392
And as a product manager,
I like this slide a lot.

00:42:15.392 --> 00:42:17.850
And hopefully as a customer--
as a user of BigQuery Storage

00:42:17.850 --> 00:42:18.770
API--

00:42:18.770 --> 00:42:20.340
you'll like it as well.

00:42:20.340 --> 00:42:21.900
So it's in beta.

00:42:21.900 --> 00:42:23.340
It's out now.

00:42:23.340 --> 00:42:25.320
Go start using it.

00:42:25.320 --> 00:42:28.635
There are still some things that
we want to do to get it to GA.

00:42:28.635 --> 00:42:29.760
But it's already out there.

00:42:29.760 --> 00:42:31.260
It's already highly useful.

00:42:31.260 --> 00:42:32.370
So how do you use it?

00:42:32.370 --> 00:42:35.880
Well, one is if you
have Dataproc running--

00:42:35.880 --> 00:42:37.320
or Hadoop or Spark--

00:42:37.320 --> 00:42:39.600
we have released
and are continuing

00:42:39.600 --> 00:42:42.270
to release a number of
connectors-- so for example

00:42:42.270 --> 00:42:46.230
Hive, MapReduce, Spark
SQL, or you can actually

00:42:46.230 --> 00:42:49.470
even just create a
Pandas data frame that

00:42:49.470 --> 00:42:51.510
is powered by BigQuery Storage.

00:42:51.510 --> 00:42:53.520
This is really
powerful because--

00:42:53.520 --> 00:42:56.490
for one-- the patterns
I've described before,

00:42:56.490 --> 00:42:57.960
you no longer have to do that.

00:42:57.960 --> 00:43:01.200
And two is you don't
need to sample your data

00:43:01.200 --> 00:43:02.880
and reduce it all
on Ubiquity storage

00:43:02.880 --> 00:43:05.130
before loading into Pandas.

00:43:05.130 --> 00:43:07.650
You simply have a
data frame that runs

00:43:07.650 --> 00:43:10.160
on top of BigQuery Storage.

00:43:10.160 --> 00:43:11.910
The next one is if
you're using Dataflow--

00:43:11.910 --> 00:43:14.310
which is an incredibly powerful,
unified batch and stream

00:43:14.310 --> 00:43:15.510
processing engine--

00:43:15.510 --> 00:43:18.240
you can read data out
of BigQuery Storage.

00:43:18.240 --> 00:43:20.490
Do some enrichment from
third-party sources--

00:43:20.490 --> 00:43:21.660
maybe Pub/Sub--

00:43:21.660 --> 00:43:23.520
and put it back into
BigQuery Storage using

00:43:23.520 --> 00:43:25.110
our real-time streaming API.

00:43:25.110 --> 00:43:28.405
So now you have a real-time
ETL processing system.

00:43:28.405 --> 00:43:30.030
So the Dataflow
connector is out there.

00:43:30.030 --> 00:43:31.830
You can start using it today.

00:43:31.830 --> 00:43:34.050
Finally ODBC and JDBC--

00:43:34.050 --> 00:43:35.897
those are some good
examples of tools

00:43:35.897 --> 00:43:37.980
that could be potentially
be benefiting from this.

00:43:37.980 --> 00:43:39.640
We've already updated
the JDBC driver,

00:43:39.640 --> 00:43:41.520
and ODBC is coming very shortly.

00:43:41.520 --> 00:43:43.470
You just get a quantum
leap of performance

00:43:43.470 --> 00:43:45.960
if you're using any third-party
tool that relies on mass

00:43:45.960 --> 00:43:47.850
or you're programmatically
connecting

00:43:47.850 --> 00:43:51.763
BigQuery using ODBC or JDBC.

00:43:51.763 --> 00:43:53.430
And finally it's--
as Seth pointed out--

00:43:53.430 --> 00:43:54.000
an API.

00:43:54.000 --> 00:43:55.320
So you can start using it.

00:43:55.320 --> 00:43:57.990
Just go and start building
code on top of it.

00:43:57.990 --> 00:44:00.180
We'll have client libraries
and all the good stuff

00:44:00.180 --> 00:44:03.830
that you used to relying on.

00:44:03.830 --> 00:44:05.970
And we'll continue to
expand on this ecosystem.

00:44:05.970 --> 00:44:08.330
So as Seth pointed out, if
you have some requirements--

00:44:08.330 --> 00:44:10.038
if you're interested
in other things that

00:44:10.038 --> 00:44:12.610
could work on top of BigQuery
Storage API, please let us

00:44:12.610 --> 00:44:13.110
know.

00:44:13.110 --> 00:44:15.860
We're really excited
to build all that.

00:44:15.860 --> 00:44:18.890
And so I'll kind of
give you a whirlwind

00:44:18.890 --> 00:44:21.950
of the different patterns
that are possible here.

00:44:21.950 --> 00:44:25.590
And you saw some of
them live with PLAID.

00:44:25.590 --> 00:44:27.110
But this is just a sampling.

00:44:27.110 --> 00:44:29.910
This is not everything
that we can give you.

00:44:29.910 --> 00:44:32.900
But I'm really excited to
tell you that that one pattern

00:44:32.900 --> 00:44:36.500
of your data being in BigQuery
Storage is now-- we feel like--

00:44:36.500 --> 00:44:38.750
very unique and very powerful.

00:44:38.750 --> 00:44:40.250
And we're looking
forward to hearing

00:44:40.250 --> 00:44:41.480
how our customers using this.

00:44:41.480 --> 00:44:45.060
We already have a couple
of users of this API--

00:44:45.060 --> 00:44:47.660
Mixpanel and Taplytics-- which
are kind of similar to PLAID.

00:44:47.660 --> 00:44:52.010
They're marketing
analytics providers.

00:44:52.010 --> 00:44:53.450
And they're using this at scale.

00:44:53.450 --> 00:44:57.360
And it has opened up a
new way of doing things.

00:44:57.360 --> 00:45:00.000
It's much simpler
than the old ways.

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:03.140
It allows a cleaner
architecture of general.

